/* Short concept explanations, separate from the project questions. */
const V2_LESSONS={
0:[['Your meaning','Start with the message and the point you want the reader to take away.','Write the meaning in one plain sentence.'],['A reader who agrees','An intended reading broadly accepts the meaning you wanted to convey.','Describe how a supportive reader might understand it.'],['A reader who partly agrees','A negotiated reading accepts part of the message but adds a “yes, but…” from the reader’s own situation.','Make the reservation specific.'],['A fair objection','An oppositional reading challenges the message through another reasonable perspective.','State the strongest fair objection.'],['Close the gap','Compare the readings before revising the words, context or terms.','Record what you changed and why.']],
1:[['The visible request','The brief states a task, may name a solution and presents claims as true. These are different things.','Separate the request, named solution and claims you can check.'],['Assumptions','An assumption is something the requested solution needs you to believe before it will work.','Name what would have to be true and how to check it.'],['The opposite option','Reversing a request makes its direction easier to question. You do not have to choose the opposite.','Ask what the reversal reveals.'],['Interests and losses','The interest is why someone wants the request. The loss is what affected people could lose.','Look at where the desired outcome and the stakes meet.'],['The real problem','A useful problem statement says what needs to improve, for whom and why. It is still a proposal to test.','Use the request, interest and losses to write it.']],
2:[['Your view','A view is an interpretation of the problem, not the problem itself.','State the problem and how you currently see it.'],['Another reasonable view','Someone else may interpret the same situation differently without being irrational.','Describe their view fairly before comparing it.'],['Deductive reasoning','Apply an accepted rule to this case. A conclusion follows only if the rule and the case’s conditions hold.','Check the rule and conclusion for both views.'],['Inductive reasoning','Repeated examples suggest a pattern. The conclusion remains limited by the cases you have.','Compare the patterns supporting both views.'],['Abductive reasoning','Choose a plausible explanation for what you observe, while keeping other explanations available.','Compare what each explanation accounts for.'],['Which view holds up?','Compare the support rather than choosing the loudest or earliest explanation.','Say which view fits best and why, or what is still undecided.'],['What would change your mind?','A specific observation that could overturn your view makes the assumption testable.','Name a result you would actually recognise.']],
3:[['The proposed action','Start with what you want the team to do. The ladder helps judge how much support that recommendation has.','State the recommendation before sorting evidence.'],['Someone said it','One interview, comment or stakeholder’s account tells you one person’s reported view.','Keep the source and context beside it.'],['Many said it','Many interviews, a survey or reviews may show a pattern in reported views.','Check who was represented and who was missing.'],['We saw it','Observation, analytics or usage data show behaviour in a context. Timing alone does not establish a cause.','Say what was observed and how.'],['We tested it','A prototype test, A/B comparison or pilot investigates a claim under stated conditions. It comes closer to proof, but still has limits.','Name the test and what it does and does not establish.'],['The failure check','A premortem imagines the recommendation failed and asks what went wrong.','Use the possible failure to expose risks.'],['An honest recommendation','State an action, its benefit, supporting evidence, remaining uncertainty and a change-of-mind condition.','Keep the action as narrow as the support permits.']],
4:[['Said','The intended story needs a clear sentence before you can check whether the piece carries it.','State the message in one line.'],['Seen','A claim that the story is visible needs somewhere specific to point.','Name the element or slide carrying it.'],['Picture first','Removing the words tests what the visual structure itself communicates.','Record what remains clear or disappears.'],['Stranger test','A real person’s unprompted account reveals what the piece communicates without your explanation.','Record their words. Label AI readings as simulations.'],['Promise kept','The finished piece should still honour what was agreed in the brief or pitch.','Compare it with the earlier promise.']],
5:[['Decided','A decision record starts with what the meeting actually agreed, not every idea discussed.','Confirm the decision against the notes.'],['Why','Reasons and criteria let the next person understand and revisit the choice.','Separate stated reasons from your interpretation.'],['Rejected','A record of alternatives prevents the next person from repeating a settled discussion without knowing why.','Name what was considered and why it was turned down.'],['Still open','Unresolved details need an owner and a next check, rather than invented agreement.','Record what remains open and who will follow up.'],['How the discussion happened','People should have room to explain their thinking before it is judged.','Name whose explanation still needs to be heard.']],
6:[['Advantage','People need to see a useful difference from what they already have.','Score whether this is better for them.'],['Fit','An idea is easier to adopt when it suits existing habits, values and systems.','Check fit in their context.'],['Simplicity','Ease of understanding and use affects adoption. A high score means simple, not complex.','Find the effort you can remove.'],['Trial','A small, reversible trial lets people learn before making a larger commitment.','Describe what they could try first.'],['Visibility','People need to recognise the result, not only hear a promise of value.','Name the visible sign that it helped.']],
7:[['Why this matrix?','Power is someone’s ability to shape or block a decision. Interest is how much its outcome matters to them. The two together help you plan involvement.','Name the decision and the affected groups.'],['Keep satisfied','High power and low interest: this group can shape the decision but may not want every detail.','Keep the outcome and risks clear without unnecessary updates.'],['Engage closely','High power and high interest: the group can shape the decision and cares about its effects.','Involve them in choices and agree how to work together.'],['Monitor','Low power and low interest: watch for changes without creating unnecessary demands.','Keep an accessible route for updates or feedback.'],['Keep informed','Low power and high interest: the group feels the effects even if it cannot approve or block the decision.','Explain changes and create a way to be heard.']],
8:[['Start with an ask','An influence cue supports an action someone can choose. If there is no ask, examine the message with Reading Check or Trust Triangle.','Name the requested action.'],['Reciprocity','A real contribution or useful help can make an invitation easier to consider. It does not create a debt that removes choice.','Turn it up by making the useful contribution clear. Turn it down by removing obligation.'],['Commitment and consistency','A freely chosen earlier commitment can help someone recognise a next step that fits it.','Turn it up by connecting to their stated commitment. Turn it down by removing guilt about changing their mind.'],['Social proof','Relevant people’s real experiences can reduce uncertainty about a choice.','Turn it up with checkable examples. Turn it down by removing a crowd claim that hides differences.'],['Liking','Respect and a real connection can help a conversation. Flattery is not evidence.','Turn it up with genuine common ground. Turn it down by removing forced familiarity.'],['Authority','Relevant expertise and credentials can support confidence in an ask. They do not replace reasons.','Turn it up with checkable expertise. Turn it down by dropping an irrelevant title.'],['Scarcity','A real limit or deadline may matter to the decision. A manufactured limit adds pressure.','Turn it up by stating the actual constraint. Turn it down by removing needless urgency.'],['Unity','A real shared identity can make an invitation feel relevant. It should not punish people for declining.','Turn it up through a meaningful shared purpose. Turn it down by removing “people like us must…” language.'],['Persuasion or pressure?','A fair ask uses a real cue, can withstand being explained and leaves an easy way to say no.','Check the three fair-play questions and revise.']],
9:[['Limits and target','A limit is the point you will not pass. A target is what you would like to achieve. Both can use money, time, scope or another condition.','Keep the two separate.'],['Is there an overlap?','An overlap exists only when compatible limits and units leave room for agreement. Unknown limits mean unknown overlap.','Do not invent the other side’s limit.'],['Priorities by issue','One issue may matter much more to one side than the other. That difference can create a useful trade.','Rank each issue on both sides.'],['Choose a posture','Hold firm when it matters to you, give way when it matters mainly to them, work strategically when both care, or avoid a low-value issue.','Choose a posture for each issue.'],['Your plan B','BATNA is what you will do if no agreement is reached. It gives you a comparison for the offer on the table.','Name a credible alternative.']],
10:[['Ethos','Ethos is why this audience should find the speaker credible and trustworthy on this subject.','Look for relevant experience, honesty and checkable credentials.'],['Pathos','Pathos connects the message with what the audience feels and cares about. It is more than adding an emotional phrase.','Check whether the message recognises their stakes.'],['Logos','Logos is the reasoning and evidence connecting the claim to the conclusion.','Check whether the support actually justifies the claim.'],['Go deeper','The trust equation compares credibility, reliability and intimacy with self-orientation. It is a reflection prompt, not a diagnostic score.','Use it when you need to examine how the relationship supports the message.']],
11:[['Catch the claim','Keep what was said separate from what you believe about the speaker.','Paste the original claim.'],['Bad logic or a bias?','Bad logic is a flaw in how an argument is built. A bias is a recurring distortion in how we judge. Several can appear together.','Compare the two labelled decks.'],['Name the trap carefully','A trap label needs a reason that fits this argument. Similar labels do different jobs.','Use the definition and compare the related traps.'],['Repair the argument','A useful repair narrows the claim, adds missing alternatives or proposes a test.','Write a more defensible version.']],
12:v2Beats.map(([title,question,meaning])=>[title,meaning,question]),
13:[['Two sides, one decision','Compare how two sides react to the same message or change. The sides may not value the same things.','Name the sides and the issue.'],['Ethics','This lens asks whether the side thinks the decision is right and fair.','Describe the fairness judgement and why.'],['Emotion','This lens asks how the decision feels and what valued experience it changes.','Keep stated feelings separate from guesses.'],['Economics','This lens asks what the decision costs or returns in money, time, effort or access.','Name the practical gains and losses.'],['Weight the drivers','Each side gets 100 points. The points show what drives the view, not whether it is positive or negative.','Find the biggest gap and open with what matters most to them.']],
14:[['Start with one decision and one person','An empathy read is about a person’s view of a particular situation. It is not a personality diagnosis.','Name the decision and the stakeholder.'],['Think','A belief may guide the person’s view even when they do not say it out loud.','Treat an unstated belief as an inference to check.'],['Feel','A feeling is the person’s emotional response to the decision.','Distinguish a feeling they named from a feeling you guessed.'],['Care','A priority is what matters to them beyond the action you want them to take.','Consider time, money, fairness, belonging or another value.'],['Say','Words record how a person reports or presents their experience.','Keep the quote or identify a paraphrase.'],['Do','Behaviour is what they actually did in context. It can differ from the stated view.','Record what you observed; do not assume the motive.'],['Our truth and their truth','Bring the five lenses together into a perspective to check with them. Their view can differ from yours without either being a complete account.','Ask what needs confirmation or correction.']]
};
